                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_constraint_right: `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> Human and structural capital are failing together — the architects left AND nothing was documented, and the units are siloed. Refilling roles alone would just refill the leak; you've named the system, not just the symptom.`,
                    coach_r1_hiring:        `<strong>You're refilling the leak, not fixing it.</strong> Replacing the lost architects with senior hires costs ~€1.6M, but your structural capital is still broken — nothing documented, units still siloed — so the new hires will leave the same way and take knowledge with them. Are you fixing the system or just the symptom?`,
                    coach_r1_relational:    `<strong>Relational capital matters, but it isn't the binding constraint.</strong> Co-innovation with customers and universities is thin, yes — but Sendoa's most urgent gaps are inside: undocumented know-how and siloed units. Fix the internal leak first.`,
                    coach_r1_even:          `<strong>'Everything is equally weak' is a non-diagnosis.</strong> Intellectual capital fails in specific places. If you can't say which component binds, you can't target the intervention — and you'll spread your €5M thin in Round 2.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the human + structural gap as the binding constraint AND weighted the urgent, irreversible risk — the tacit knowledge walking out with two more retirements. That is exactly the lens that tells you where to concentrate the €5M.`,
                    coach_r1_platformfirst: `<strong>The platform isn't the urgent risk.</strong> A relaunch can wait a quarter; two architects retiring in 18 months cannot. If they leave before you capture their know-how, that ~€2.1M of tacit knowledge is gone permanently. Weight the irreversible loss first.`,
                    coach_r1_revenue:       `<strong>Chasing the revenue number directly skips the cause.</strong> Output is a lagging result of mobilized knowledge, not a lever you pull. Worry about the tacit knowledge walking out the door now — the revenue follows when the knowledge flows.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>That's how the knowledge walks out.</strong> 'Take the year to study it' means the two retiring architects leave undocumented. The most urgent risk is irreversible — capture the fragile tacit knowledge before it's gone.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigour:     `<strong>A gut-feel gap map will misdirect the €5M.</strong> Without ranking the gaps by impact and urgency you can't justify where to concentrate — and you'll fund the comfortable options instead of the binding one.`,
                    coach_r2_nocapture:     `<strong>You under-funded the one urgent, irreversible item.</strong> Expert-capture pairs the departing architects with successors before they retire. Starve it and ~€2.1M of tacit knowledge walks out undocumented — communities and rotation won't capture that in time.`,
                    coach_r2_evenspread:    `<strong>You funded a little of everything.</strong> Each programme is now under-resourced — the apprenticeship has no protected time, the platform has no curation staff, open innovation has one part-time lead. None reaches the adoption threshold that creates impact. With one year, where should you have concentrated?`,
                    coach_r2_focused:       `<strong>That's a focused plan.</strong> You concentrated on the urgent expert-capture and a couple of high-impact mechanisms rather than sprinkling the budget. Now the incentives in Round 3 decide whether the knowledge actually flows.`,
                    coach_r2_urgentfirst:   `<strong>Urgent-first sequencing is right.</strong> Capturing the at-risk tacit knowledge now, while building the rest in parallel, respects the one thing you cannot get back — the departing architects' undocumented know-how.`,
                    coach_r2_platformseq:   `<strong>Platform-first repeats the 2021 instinct.</strong> Leading with the system means the architects may retire before you capture their knowledge, and the platform still has no incentive to drive adoption. Lead with the irreversible loss.`,
                    coach_r3_sweetspot:     `<strong>That incentive weight is in the sweet spot.</strong> Heavy enough that sharing and reuse count toward bonus and promotion — so knowledge actually flows — without swamping the drive to ship. This is the HRM–knowledge link the 2021 platform was missing.`,
                    coach_r3_toolow:        `<strong>This is the 2021 failure repeating.</strong> With almost no reward for sharing, engineers keep shipping their own unit's features and the platform sits unused again. A mechanism without an incentive to use it dies — exactly what happened last time.`,
                    coach_r3_toohigh:       `<strong>You've over-rewarded sharing and crippled delivery.</strong> Make it 40% of every engineer's bonus and they write wiki articles instead of shipping product. Sharing has to lift innovation output, not replace the work. Dial it back to a balance.`,
                    coach_r3_reuse:         `<strong>Rewarding reuse and impact is exactly right.</strong> You're paying for documented know-how that others demonstrably build on — not for raw posts. That ties the incentive to innovation outcome, not vanity activity, with a sensible weight behind it.`,
                    coach_r3_activity:      `<strong>Rewarding activity breeds vanity metrics.</strong> Pay for posts and café sessions and you'll get 1,200 posts and no more innovation. Reward the reuse and impact that move output, not the volume of knowledge filed.`,
                    coach_r3_shiponly:      `<strong>Leaving incentives unchanged guarantees the relaunch fails.</strong> If engineers are still rewarded only for shipping their own features, why would adoption be different this time? This is precisely why the 2021 platform died.`,
                    coach_r3_noprotect:     `<strong>No protected time, no curation owner — no adoption.</strong> Telling engineers to document and mentor with no freed time and nobody owning curation reproduces the 2021 failure. Adoption, not purchase, is what failed last time.`,
                    coach_r4_accelerate:    `<strong>Redirecting to accelerate expert-capture is the right call.</strong> An architect retiring early is the irreversible risk materialising. Moving the uncommitted €1.0M to capture that knowledge before it walks is exactly the urgency-weighting this whole plan rests on.`,
                    coach_r4_moreplatform:  `<strong>More money won't fix an adoption problem.</strong> Pouring the rest into the platform when adoption is lagging is the 2021 mistake at scale — the issue was never the tool, it was the incentive to use it. And the architect is still about to leave undocumented.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>Staying the course as the architect leaves is how the knowledge walks.</strong> The plan won't 'catch up on its own' — the early retirement is the irreversible loss you were warned about. Redirect now or lose that tacit knowledge for good.`,
                    coach_r4_outcomes:      `<strong>Measuring innovation outcomes is the discipline the board needs.</strong> New-idea rate, time-to-innovation and new-product revenue tell them the knowledge is turning into innovation — not that people are busy. That is the single signal that proves this worked.`,
                    coach_r4_mixed:         `<strong>A mixed dashboard is better than activity alone, but it blurs the signal.</strong> Activity counts let people hide behind 1,200 posts. Lead with the outcome KPIs — new-product revenue and time-to-innovation — and let activity be supporting detail at most.`,
                    coach_r4_activity:      `<strong>Activity metrics are knowledge theatre.</strong> 1,200 posts and 60 café sessions show effort, not result. New-product revenue is still stuck — you're measuring busyness, not innovation. Which KPI tells the board this actually worked?`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's a board-ready, honest case.</strong> Output recovering toward 30%, the captured tacit knowledge, and adoption that's real because the incentives changed — that's a recovery that lasts, not a number propped up by activity.`,
                    coach_r4_optimistic:    `<strong>Selling the activity surge is a short-lived win.</strong> Emphasise posts and sessions and project a fast rebound, and the board sees through it the first quarter output doesn't move. Lead with the outcome, not the busyness.`,
                    coach_r4_buy:           `<strong>Conceding to buy-not-build dismantles Sendoa's R&D identity.</strong> That is exactly the board pivot the escalation warned about — and it throws away the intellectual capital you were given a year and €5M to rebuild. You had a mobilization plan; defend it.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
